            "type": "extended",
            "marks": 3,
            "source": {
              "qp": 10,
              "ms": 11
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "From the 1920s until the 1970s, insulin obtained from the bodies of animals was used to treat diabetes. From the 1970s, recombinant human insulin was used instead.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 10
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Explain the advantages of using recombinant human insulin to treat people with diabetes.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 10
                }
              }
            ],
            "topics": [
              {
                "ref": "19.2(1)",
                "role": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "markScheme": {
              "instruction": "<i>any </i><b><i>three</i></b><i> from: </i><br><i>(protein made in, bacteria</i> <i>/</i> <i>yeast, so) </i>",
              "guidance": [],
              "points": [
                {
                  "n": 1,
                  "text": "can be made in, large / unlimited, quantities <b>; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 2,
                  "text": "cheap to make / low cost / cost-efficient <b>; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 3,
                  "text": "does not, harm / involve, animals / pigs / cows <b>; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 4,
                  "text": "no risk of (named) disease, transfer / infection<b> ; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 5,
                  "text": "<i>(sequence identical to human sequence so)</i><br>does not cause, allergy / inflammatory response / immune response / antibody production <b>; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 6,
                  "text": "acts more rapidly <b>; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 7,
                  "text": "do not, develop tolerance to it / need larger doses <b>;</b>"
                }
              ],
              "source": {
                "ms": 11
              },
              "marks": 3
            },
            "er": [],
            "aiSupplement": {
              "explanation": [
                {
                  "text": "Production in microorganisms makes supply scalable. A human amino acid sequence also reduces the risk that injected insulin is recognised as foreign by the immune system.",
                  "refs": [
                    "ms:a.ii#1",
                    "ms:a.ii#2",
                    "ms:a.ii#3",
                    "ms:a.ii#4",
                    "ms:a.ii#5",
                    "ms:a.ii#6",
                    "ms:a.ii#7",
                    "syllabus:19.2(1)"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reasoningChain": [
                "Separate advantages of production from advantages of the human protein sequence.",
                "Explain each benefit in the context of treatment."
              ],
              "inferredMistakes": []
            },
            "label": "(ii)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "key": "b",
        "label": "(b)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 10
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Insulin is composed of two polypeptide chains, the A chain and the B chain, that are linked by disulfide bonds.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 10
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Variations in amino acid sequence occur:",
            "source": {
              "qp": 10
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "list",
            "items": [
              "in the insulin molecules of different animals",
              "in new versions of human insulin that have been engineered to control blood glucose concentration more effectively than normal recombinant human insulin. These new versions of human insulin are called analogues."
            ],
            "source": {
              "qp": 10
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Table 4.1 shows the amino acid positions where variation occurs in different animal and human analogue insulin molecules. The dashes indicate a missing amino acid.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 11
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "figure",
            "caption": "Table 4.1",
            "src": "figures/q4_table4_1.png",
            "alt": "Table comparing amino acids at ten positions in human, cow, pig, cat, short-acting analogue and long-acting analogue insulin.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 11
            },
            "id": "table4.1"
          }
        ],
        "parts": [
          {
            "key": "b.i",
            "type": "short",
            "marks": 1,
            "source": {
              "qp": 11,
              "ms": 11
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Cats with diabetes can be successfully treated with insulin injections. Cat insulin is <b>not</b> available, but vets can choose from the other types of insulin shown in Table 4.1.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 11
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Identify the type of insulin that is most suitable for treating cats.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 11
                }
              }
            ],
            "topics": [
